          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "**Swelling in the ankles is worth a call to her clinician, and the warning signs below need immediate help.** A screening check of your description found no emergency pattern, but that check is not a clearance. It only covers a fixed list of presentations.\n\n**Get urgent help now if she has any of these:** breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that wakes her and does not ease when she sits upright; chest pain; fainting; a very fast or irregular pulse with symptoms; new confusion; or pink frothy sputum with severe breathlessness, which is an emergency. Rapid weight gain over a few days also needs urgent assessment. (Source: \"Heart failure,\" General clinical reference.)\n\n**Ankle swelling by itself:** The heart failure summary lists recognizing early fluid retention as part of routine self-management. The diet and fluid reference says daily weights are the usual early signal, and people are often asked to contact their clinician before breathlessness or swelling gets worse. If she has a written action plan with weight thresholds, that plan is the guide to follow. (Source: \"Diet and fluid in heart failure,\" General clinical reference and cardiology educational summary.)\n\n**The four drug classes:** The guidance I found says heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (40% or below) is treated with four foundational drug classes, started together or in quick succession. The passage I retrieved did not list the four classes or describe what each does, so I cannot tell you more from these sources. Her clinician or pharmacist can explain her specific medicines. Please do not change, skip, or add any medicine based on this summary. Potassium supplements and salt substitutes can interact with heart failure medicines, so check with her clinician before she uses them. (Source: \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes,\" cardiology society guidance summary.)\n\n**Suggested next step:** Contact her heart team or clinician today about the new swelling. Mention the four medicines and any recent weight change. If any warning sign above appears, call emergency services rather than waiting.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
